    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe feature is well-placed overall \u2014 persisting the cap on `Owner` fits the existing idiom (its siblings `possibleDuplicateOf`, `bulkSignupWarning`, `namesakeCount` are all controller-derived fields the mapper/DTO consume), applying the cap inside `formatMembershipLevel` is the right unit since that method already derives the level, and the inline cap derivation in the controller matches the altitude of the surrounding create logic. One structural issue, though:\n\n**Duplication between `findHouseholdMembers` and `isHouseholdInUse` (`ClinicServiceImpl.java:317-340`).** The new `findHouseholdMembers` is a near-verbatim copy of `isHouseholdInUse`:\n\n- both guard on `householdId == null`,\n- both do `ownerRepository.findAll().stream().filter(id != null).filter(!deleted)`,\n- the only difference is `.anyMatch(householdId.equals(...))` vs `.filter(householdId.equals(...)).collect(...)`.\n\nThis is the exact household-membership predicate (same household id, persisted, non-deleted) now written in two places. If one is ever refined (e.g. the deleted/persisted filtering changes), the two will drift. Collapse them: have `isHouseholdInUse` delegate to the richer method \u2014\n\n```java\npublic boolean isHouseholdInUse(Owner owner) throws DataAccessException {\n    return !findHouseholdMembers(owner).isEmpty();\n}\n```\n\nso the membership rule lives in a single place and `isHouseholdInUse` reads as the boolean projection of it.\n\nMinor (optional): the `max() + 1` household-ceiling derivation in the controller (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java:199-206`) is a bit more domain computation than the one-line service calls around it. It's within the method's existing altitude so not required, but a `clinicService.findMembershipLevelCap(owner)` (or similar) would keep the \"one above the household max\" rule beside `findHouseholdMembers` in the service layer rather than reconstructing it from the member collection in the controller.",
